<Wiki
  title="Scope"
  description="What puv does not do: Composer itself, plugins, compiling PHP, and embedding the runtime."
>
  <p class="kicker">Wiki</p>
  <h1>Scope</h1>
  <p>
    puv is one Rust binary. It installs the PHP runtime, resolves Packagist packages, and runs the
    project. Composer package names, version constraints, and the Composer v2 metadata API are the
    way in. The resolver, cache, environment, and CLI are puv’s own.
  </p>
  <p>The binary does not shell out to Composer, and it does not load Composer plugins.</p>

  <h2>What this is not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>It does not start <code>composer</code>, and it does not link Composer as a library.</li>
    <li>It does not run scripts from a downloaded <code>composer.json</code>.</li>
    <li>It does not load Composer plugins.</li>
    <li>It does not compile PHP. Runtimes are prebuilt StaticPHP CLI builds.</li>
    <li>
      It does not embed the PHP runtime inside a PHAR.
      <a href="/wiki/commands/package"><code>puv package</code></a> packs the application and its
      dependencies. The runtime stays outside.
    </li>
    <li>Published runtime targets are Linux glibc only (<code>x86_64</code> and <code>aarch64</code>).</li>
    <li>
      It does not treat <code>vendor/</code> as the project layout.
      <a href="/wiki/commands/run"><code>puv run</code></a> injects <code>.puv/autoload.php</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A normal library or CLI project with a lockfile is the migration target.
    <a href="/wiki/commands/contain"><code>puv contain</code></a> copies string scripts, skips PHP
    callables and <code>@composer</code> lines, and leaves the Composer files in place until
    <code>--clean</code>. The walk for that directory is an
    <a href="/wiki/existing-project">existing project</a>.
  </p>
</Wiki>
